# Git Workflow — Shopify Theme Development

## Branch Strategy
```
main              ← mirrors live published theme (production)
├── staging       ← pre-production / QA theme
└── feature/*     ← individual feature branches
    ├── feature/custom-hero-banner
    ├── feature/sticky-header
    └── fix/cart-drawer-mobile
```

## Branch Naming
- Features: `feature/[short-description]` (e.g., `feature/custom-announcement-bar`)
- Bug fixes: `fix/[short-description]` (e.g., `fix/mobile-nav-overlap`)
- Hotfixes: `hotfix/[short-description]` (e.g., `hotfix/checkout-button-broken`)

## Commit Message Convention (Conventional Commits)
```
feat: add custom hero banner section with video support
fix: correct mobile cart drawer z-index issue
style: update product card hover animation
refactor: extract badge logic into custom-product-badge snippet
perf: lazy load collection page images below fold
a11y: add aria-label to all icon-only nav buttons
chore: update shopify.theme.toml with staging theme ID
```

## What to .gitignore
```
# Always ignore
node_modules/
.DS_Store
*.log

# Sensitive - never commit production settings
config/settings_data.json

# Shopify CLI files (generated)
.shopify/
```

## Shopify Theme Environments Workflow
- `main` branch → push to **Live Theme** (published)
- `staging` branch → push to **Staging Theme** (unpublished, for QA)
- `feature/*` branch → push to **Development Theme** (unpublished, for dev preview)

```bash
# Push current branch to dev theme
shopify theme push --theme [dev-theme-id]

# Push to staging theme
shopify theme push --theme [staging-theme-id] --environment staging

# NEVER run push without --theme on main branch accidentally
```

## Pull Request Rules
- All PRs target `staging` first — never push directly to `main`
- PR title follows commit convention
- Include before/after screenshots for visual changes
- Link to the Shopify theme preview URL in PR description
